<title>UK: Shell to set up Canada ethylene glycol plant.</title>
<headline>Shell to set up Canada ethylene glycol plant.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-02-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Shell International Chemicals Ltd said on Thursday that Shell Chemicals Canada Ltd was designing a new 400,000 tonne-per-year ethylene glycol plant to start operations in 2000.</p>
<p>In a statement released in London, Shell International Chemicals, a division of the Royal Dutch Shell Group said the plant would cost C$400 million and would be located at its Scotford site near Fort Saskatchewan, Canada.</p>
<p>Shell said Mitsubishi Chemicals Corp would be a partner in the venture. It gave no further details.</p>
<p>Ethylene glycol is used to make polyester fibres, resins and antifreeze, Shell said. Demand is growing at six percent per annum, notably from north America and the Pacific Rim nations.</p>
<p>--London Newsroom +44 171 542 4017</p>
</text>
